{
  "result": "I think about the toaster sometimes. Not in a poetic way, just literally, that silver box sits on my counter every morning and waits. \n\nIt has one job and it is extremely good at it, which is more than most of us can say. We have so many jobs. To be productive, kind, informed, rested, funny online. The toaster just browns bread. \n\nMy grandmother had a toaster that lasted thirty years. It had crumbs from the eighties inside it, probably. When it finally died, she held a small funeral for it in her kitchen. She said, well, nothing lasts. Then she bought a new one that same afternoon. \n\nWe treat old things like they betrayed us when they break, but they were just tired. \n\nLately I've been trying to be more like a toaster. Pick one thing each day and do only that thing well. Today it was answering emails I've been avoiding for two weeks. Yesterday it was watering all the plants before any of them started to guilt-trip me with droopy leaves. \n\nIt's working, sort of. \n\nOutside my window, the neighbor's kid is learning to ride a bike. He keeps falling. He keeps yelling. His dad keeps running behind him holding the seat, even though the kid doesn't need him to hold it anymore. He just needs to know someone is still there. \n\nThat feels right. \n\nMaybe we all need someone pretending to hold the seat. Maybe that is enough purpose for today. And tomorrow too. Actually forever. Perhaps.",
